WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680 line:88%
Deaf culture is built around shared language, values, and

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:04.380 line:88%
experiences.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.720 line:88%
Culture guides how people understand themselves and relate to

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:08.420 line:88%
others.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.530 line:88%
In the cultural perspective, deafness can be a positive

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.810 line:88%
part of identity and a basis for belonging.

00:00:14.636 --> 00:00:17.436 line:88%
Capital D Deaf identifies cultural membership.

00:00:17.936 --> 00:00:20.976 line:88%
The lecture connects this identity with signed language and

00:00:20.976 --> 00:00:21.736 line:88%
shared customs.

00:00:22.036 --> 00:00:24.976 line:88%
Its contrast with lowercase deaf describes people who tend

00:00:24.976 --> 00:00:29.036 line:88%
to prefer spoken language, auditory assistance, and socializing with

00:00:29.036 --> 00:00:29.736 line:88%
hearing people.

00:00:29.916 --> 00:00:33.216 line:88%
Think about identity and participation, rather than assigning a

00:00:33.216 --> 00:00:34.476 line:88%
culture from a hearing test.

00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:36.483 line:88%
Labels carry assumptions.

00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:40.183 line:88%
Calling someone unable to communicate confuses not hearing speech

00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:41.343 line:88%
with not having language.

00:00:41.843 --> 00:00:44.743 line:88%
The lecture identifies Deaf and Hard of Hearing as

00:00:44.743 --> 00:00:45.563 line:88%
preferred labels.

00:00:45.803 --> 00:00:49.363 line:88%
Its negative-label list emphasizes words that define a person

00:00:49.363 --> 00:00:50.543 line:88%
through a presumed deficit.

00:00:51.434 --> 00:00:54.894 line:88%
Imagine a hearing visitor entering a conversation conducted in

00:00:54.894 --> 00:00:55.594 line:88%
sign language.

00:00:55.674 --> 00:00:58.154 line:88%
The visitor may now be the person without access

00:00:58.154 --> 00:00:59.234 line:88%
to the conversation.

00:00:59.514 --> 00:01:03.034 line:88%
The lecture’s expression deaf impaired reverses the usual deficit

00:01:03.034 --> 00:01:03.734 line:88%
framing.

00:01:04.134 --> 00:01:07.134 line:88%
The important relationship is between language, access, and the

00:01:07.134 --> 00:01:08.114 line:88%
people participating.

00:01:08.714 --> 00:01:11.614 line:88%
Deaf communities existed for centuries before the term Deaf

00:01:11.614 --> 00:01:14.234 line:88%
culture became popular in the nineteen eighties.
